From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Wed, 23 Nov 1994 17:55:21 -0800
Subject: [#94-11-336] NANOTECH: Drexler predicts when it will happen

Gregory Sullivan writes:
>One might also argue that Drexler has a vested interest in claiming
>that nanotech will be developed soon so that his various institutes
>will be funded. This is inconsistent with my impression that Drexler
>is a brilliant man who greatly values the truth and wants to genuinely
>understand and influence the future.

Brilliance, a value for truth, and a desire to understand can be a
poor match for powerful self-interest.  You should excersize extra
skepticism regarding anyone's self-interested claims, myself included,
regardless of how saintly that person seems to you.

Drexler and company have offered detailed defenses of their technical
conclusions and have submitted them to ample criticism - they pass the
usual tests of rigor and care and can be relied on in my opinion.
Their social conclusions, regarding rates of progress, suddenness of
transitions, the need to restrict access to nanotech, expectations
regarding future per capita wealth, etc. have not been accompanied by
similar detailed defenses submitted to ample criticism, and so cannot
be relied on in the same way.

Ed Regis writes:
>Apparently, some people love to make predictions.  After having written two
>books on "futuristic" themes (one of them being nanotechnology), one thing I
>have learned is the futility of making predictions about anything not covered
>by the laws of the hard sciences.  Quite probably, most of the predictions
>that have ever been made have been wrong, and I myself avoid them like the
>plague.  

Completely avoiding any predictions seems to me as much an over
reaction as that by those who uncritically consume any remotely
plausible stories about what might happen.  We must make decisions, so
we can and must form expectations about the future, and engineering
and the soft sciences contain valuable relevant information to help
inform these expectations.

Robin Hanson, Near-Professional Social Scientist




